      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to choose 3 more players from the remaining 10 players (since Ace and Zeppo are already in the lineup). This is a combination problem, where the order of selection does not matter.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 players from 10 is given by the combination formula:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial, which is the product of all positive integers up to that number.\n\nHere, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the factorials. Notice that \\( 10! = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7! \\). The \\( 7! \\) terms cancel out in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{3! \\times 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\( 3! \\):\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{6}\n\\]\n\nNext, we perform the multiplication and division:\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n90 \\times 8 = 720\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{720}{6} = 120\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to choose 3 players from the remaining 10 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{120}\n\\]\n\nSo, there are \\(\\boxed{120}\\) different starting lineups possible.",
